      "generated_answer": "According to Rule 17.7 [Doc: core_rules], a group can change its group identity and position directly opposite an opposing group by announcing a Lateral Group Transfer and playing a Movement card in the sideways mode. However, the rule does not explicitly state that the groups involved must be labeled with the next sequential identity chits in order to perform a Lateral Group Transfer. The rule mentions that such a transfer can only occur if there is no other friendly group in that position at the start of the turn, and adjacent groups can exchange identities and positions simultaneously if both groups are capable of movement.\n\nThe rule specifies that to perform a Lateral Group Transfer, a player must announce the intention and play a Movement card sideways, but the rule does not mention any strict sequential identity chit requirement beyond the mentioned conditions.\n\nRegarding your question about whether the player can change their mind after announcing the intention: According to the provided rule, once a player announces the intention to perform a Lateral Group Transfer, and the conditions (such as the Movement card being played sideways) are met, the transfer is initiated. The rule does not explicitly state that a player can change their mind after announcing the intention, but it also does not provide a clear process for retracting such an announcement. Given the rules as they are provided, there's no explicit provision for changing one's mind after announcing a Lateral Group Transfer, implying that once conditions are met, the action stands.\n\nTo address your specific scenario in General Vol 21 Number 1, if the American player plays a sideways Movement card on his Group C and announces it as a Lateral Group Transfer, it is valid for the player to do so regardless of the specific identity chits held by the German player. The rule does not dictate that the opposing group must have the immediately preceding or following identity chits to allow a transfer.\n\nIn summary, the rule allows a player to perform a Lateral Group Transfer without a strict identity chit requirement, as long as the conditions are met. The player can announce the intention to perform a Lateral Group Transfer and follow the necessary procedure (playing a Movement card sideways) to initiate the transfer.\n\n[Rule: 17.7] [Doc: core_rules]\n\nThis text has changed across versions. Would you like to know how it worked in a specific version, or how it has evolved over time?",
      "ground_truth_answer": "In the General Vol 21 Number 1 scenario A series replay, on turn 5 the American plays a sideways movement card on his group C \"as a Lateral Group Transfer\". But given the German player only has groups at identity chit A and B isn't this illegal? Rule 17.7 first sentence says \"... directly opposite an opposing group ...\"\n\nNo, it says \"A group capable of movement can change its group identity and thereby its position directly opposing an enemy group....\"  All it is saying if you move group from B to C, the directly opposing group would now be C--if it exists.  If the opponent  does not have a Group C, then there would be no directly opposing group to your Group C.  (Just like there would be if each side set up with a different number of groups at start.)\n\nIt is not stating a requirement, but a change of status of \"directly opposing\" groups.\n\nThe rule states the player announces the intention to perform a lateral group transfer so presumably if the player doesn't make the announcement at the time of playing the movement card sideways he can't subsequently do so on a future turn (using that same played movement card) even if the necessary conditions still exist to make such a move? \n\nCorrect, if the intention to change a group's ID is not made when playing the movement cards, when group enters terrain, the same Group ID remains.  Very common if you want to get into better terrain.\n\nWhere a game is played without aid of voice or text chat I guess you might agree that a lateral group transfer announcement was accompanied by putting down the new group identity chit in the adjacent column and taken away if the attempt failed.\n\nA common technique in ftf play is to place the new ID on the Movement card until new terrain is accepted or rejected (which would cancel the transfer).\n\nI wonder whether having declared the intention to lateral group transfer a player can change their mind? \n\nI would say no per errata on Rule 5:  Movement cards not played directly to the discard pile cannot voluntarily be cancelled.  (Just like you can't rotate a Movement card from advancing to sideways because you change your mind about advancing.)\n\nEdit: Looking through past posts I see I have broached this issue before! See https://boardgamegeek.com/thread/1342845/article/35597336#35597336 where no conclusion was reached :)\n\nNo big deal, that thread started 11 years ago and you added your question 5 years after that.",
