      "query": "Prisoners/guarding (32): Taking a deep dive into rules I never see used in the games I've played of UF.  \n\nFrom a re-formatted rule book that embeds the Q/A.  \n\n32.12 & 16.42 By capturing a man and bringing him into a group, and\nthen stopping in suitable terrain, does the attacker satisfy the Relative\nRange 5 requirement of the Victory Conditions, if, in so doing, it\neliminates the only Relative Range 5 enemy group?\nA. Yes. Note, this rule is in effect to prevent a player from routing men\nto avoid victory by the opponent.\n\n---\n\nI don't understand the answer.  Players can't rout men of their own volition that I know of.  I could see someone voluntarily panic one of their PCs to eliminate a RR5 situation.  Is that what it's talking about?\n---\n\nPrisoners, once taken, are placed\nbeneath the card of any one man in the capturing\ngroup of the capturing player's choice. That man\nmay be pinned, wounded, or even unarmed but he\nmay not be the designated assistant crewman for any weapon.\n\n---\n\nIt's not \"that man may NOT be pinned\"  Pinned PCs can actually do something?  The only thing is guarding?\n\n--\n\nIf the designated assistant crewman is the only possible choice...\n\n--\n\nHow is that possible?  Who is he crewing for if he's the only choice?\n\n--\n\nA player may change guards automatically as the sole Action of a group for that turn provided\nthe new guard is not currently pinned.\n\n--\n\nThe 2nd paragraph says yes for pinned PCs as guards and the last says no.  I see there's a, very, slight difference in the initiation of the guard duty between the two but I don't see the point.",
